<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ælla of Northumbria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/867">867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ælla (died 867) was king of Northumbria in the 860s, having displaced King Osberht. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> seized York in 866, the rival kings made peace and attacked the city together in March 867, but both were killed. Later Norse sagas made Ælla the slayer of the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> Ragnar Lodbrok and told of his gruesome death at the hands of Ragnar's sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aella-of-northumbria.jpg" alt="Ælla of Northumbria" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hugo Hamilton (1802–1871) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ragnar_Lodbroks_d%C3%B6d_by_Hugo_Hamilton.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ælla became king of Northumbria after Osberht was deposed. His reign is traditionally dated to 862 or 863, but the evidence for Northumbrian royal chronology is unreliable before 867, and it may have begun as late as 866. His ancestry is unknown. Almost nothing is known of his reign. Symeon of Durham states that Ælla seized lands at Billingham, Ileclif, Wigeclif and Crece, which belonged to the church. Most sources describe him as a tyrant and an illegitimate king, though one source calls him Osberht's brother. An Anglo-Norman genealogy gives him a daughter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelthryth">Æthelthryth</a>, through whom he was grandfather of Eadwulf of Bamburgh, who died in 913.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a>, made up mostly of Danish, Norwegian and Frisian Vikings, landed in Northumbria in mid-866 and had captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> by 21 November. According to the Historia Regum Anglorum, the dissension between Osberht and Ælla was allayed by divine counsel and other Northumbrian nobles. They united their forces and came to York on 21 March 867.</p><p>Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> seemed to flee, and the Christians, seeing this, attacked but found the Vikings the stronger party. Surrounded, the Northumbrians fought fiercely until both kings were killed, and the survivors made peace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> says both kings were slain on the spot. The Vikings then installed a puppet king, Ecgberht.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Norse sources tell a different story. According to Ragnarssona þáttr, Ælla captured Ragnar Lodbrok, a figure of Norse tradition, and put him to death in a pit of snakes, and the army of 866 was led by Ragnar's sons, Hvitserk, Björn Ironside, Sigurd Snake-in-the-Eye, Ivar the Boneless and Ubba, who avenged him by killing Ælla with the blood eagle.</p><p>Roberta Frank argued that the saga authors misunderstood kennings alluding to leaving foes face down on the battlefield, their backs torn by scavenging birds, so that the blood eagle may describe his death in battle. Ælla also appears in Chaucer's The Man of Law's Tale and was played by Frank Thring in the film The Vikings in 1958.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ælla of Northumbria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 9th century Northumbria faced the Vikings, and in 867 its rival kings Osberht and <b>Ælla</b> were killed trying to retake York from the Great Heathen Army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.95,-1.08&amp;year=857" aria-label="York on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ælla of Northumbria"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4945 5516h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4945 5516h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.95,-1.08&amp;year=857">What was here in 857 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
